{"chapter": "artin", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "07XD", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0002": "$1$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:99b8e20f57508c5b764095b9968ef0e5fb645eb7fc022c726f0071ab49fc4cc3", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:955a0d75bbe2ab10d352336f0ca3d5a00cdd1c804aa92f8a9816ae39fa1a6f4a", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn the previous section we explained how to approximate objects over\ncomplete local rings by algebraic objects. But in order to show that\na stack <MATH_0001> is an algebraic stack, we need to find smooth\n<MATH_0002>-morphisms from schemes towards <MATH_0003>. Since we are not going\nto assume a priori that <MATH_0004> has a representable diagonal, we\ncannot even speak about smooth morphisms towards <MATH_0005>. Instead,\nborrowing terminology from deformation theory, we will introduce versal\nobjects.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:ebed5aaf628223f3d009bcf07d3c825875b62976d663eb07b65d0df5c6006dd9", "unit_id": "tag:07XD:prose-0001"}
